1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,533
Every request reserves memory
for its maximum length.

2
00:00:04,533 --> 00:00:08,050
Only a few slots hold real
tokens. The rest sit idle.

3
00:00:08,050 --> 00:00:12,567
A new request is rejected,
even though memory sits idle.

4
00:00:12,567 --> 00:00:15,583
PagedAttention borrows paging
from operating systems.

5
00:00:15,583 --> 00:00:18,767
Split the sequence into small
fixed-size blocks.

6
00:00:18,767 --> 00:00:21,500
GPU memory is a pool of
same-size blocks.

7
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:28,950
A block table maps each
logical block to any free one.

8
00:00:28,950 --> 00:00:32,417
Blocks can sit anywhere. No
contiguous space needed.

9
00:00:32,417 --> 00:00:35,067
New token? Write it into the
last block.

10
00:00:35,067 --> 00:00:39,267
Block full? Claim a free
block, add a table row.

11
00:00:39,267 --> 00:00:42,967
Result: waste drops from
60-80% to under 4%.

12
00:00:42,967 --> 00:00:46,367
Same GPU, more requests served
at once.
